  add('b12-b-s09','Pathogen spotting and educational symptom links',
    'Identify teaching material and connect it to transmission or typical disease features. Symptoms overlap and cannot diagnose infection from a lesson.',
    ['Sealed prepared slides or authorised virtual images/models','Ascaris, Entamoeba, Plasmodium and ringworm-fungus references','Comparison sheet'],
    ['Identify specimen type and scale before using a key.','Compare Ascaris body form, Entamoeba cellular form, Plasmodium teaching blood-stage images and fungal hyphae/spores.','Connect each example to the relevant disease and general transmission route from the reference.','Record why symptoms alone or an unverified image are insufficient for diagnosis.'],
    ['Organism','Identification evidence','Disease association','Transmission','Limits'],
    ['Ascaris is associated with intestinal ascariasis; pathogenic Entamoeba histolytica with amoebiasis; Plasmodium with malaria; dermatophyte fungi with ringworm.','Ascariasis may cause abdominal discomfort, but many infections have few or no symptoms. Malaria commonly involves fever; amoebiasis may involve intestinal symptoms; ringworm commonly produces itchy skin lesions. These are non-specific educational associations, not a diagnostic checklist.'],
    ['Ringworm is fungal, not a worm. Do not identify Entamoeba species or malaria solely from a stylised drawing.'],slides+' Never collect blood, stool, skin scrapings or infectious material. Seek qualified healthcare for health concerns.',
    [['Ringworm is caused by…',['Dermatophyte fungi','An annelid worm','Pollen'],0,'Its common name describes a lesion pattern, not a worm.'],['Do shared symptoms establish the pathogen?',['No; clinical evaluation and appropriate tests are needed','Yes','Only online'],0,'Many illnesses share symptoms; this spotting task cannot diagnose disease.']],{references:['senior','manual','ascaris']});
  add('b12-b-s10','Lichens, root nodules and Cuscuta',
    'Compare associations by resource exchange and effects on partners. A visible association alone does not measure its benefit or cost.',
    ['Authorised lichen, legume-root-nodule and Cuscuta images/models','Hand lens for safe teacher material','Partner-interaction sheet'],
    ['Identify both partners where possible and separate visible features from inferred processes.','Observe lichen organisation and root nodules; connect them to photosynthetic and nitrogen-fixing partners respectively.','Locate Cuscuta on a host and identify attachment evidence without claiming to see molecular transfer.','Draw arrows of proposed resource transfer and state the evidence needed to test them.'],
    ['Association','Partners','Resource relationship','Visible evidence','Inference limit'],
    ['Lichens involve a fungus and photosynthetic partner; legume nodules can house nitrogen-fixing bacteria.','Cuscuta is a parasitic flowering plant that draws resources through haustoria. A nodule alone does not prove its present nitrogen-fixation rate.'],
    ['Calling every root swelling a nodule or assuming a partner benefits under all conditions.'],'Use teacher-approved material; do not spread parasitic plants or disturb protected lichens.',
    [['Cuscuta is…',['A parasitic flowering plant','A nitrogen-fixing bacterium','A mushroom'],0,'It obtains resources from a host through specialised attachments.'],['A visible nodule proves a measured fixation rate?',['No','Yes','Only if large'],0,'Activity requires physiological evidence beyond structure.']]);
  add('b12-b-s11','Homologous and analogous structures',
    'Homology concerns shared evolutionary origin; analogy concerns similar function that evolved independently. The level of comparison matters.',
    ['Authorised model/flash-card sets','Vertebrate forelimb bones','Insect and bird wing examples','Comparison diagram'],
    ['Describe function and structural arrangement separately for each pair.','Compare corresponding bones in vertebrate forelimbs before noting their different uses.','Compare flight function of bird and insect wings while recording their different structural origins.','Explain the evolutionary inference and identify the exact feature being compared.'],
    ['Pair','Function','Structural correspondence','Origin inference','Conclusion'],
    ['Human arm, whale flipper and bat forelimb share an inherited bone plan despite divergent functions.','Bird and insect wings are analogous as flight organs. Bird and bat forelimbs are homologous, while their wing specialisations require a more precise comparison.'],
    ['Equating similarity with ancestry or forgetting that the same pair can be analysed at different structural levels.'],'Use models and authorised imagery only; no animal handling.',
    [['Homology primarily concerns…',['Shared evolutionary origin','Identical current function','Matching colour'],0,'Inherited structural correspondence can persist despite different functions.'],['Why specify the feature being compared?',['Origin and function can differ at different levels','Every similarity is identical','It avoids needing evidence'],0,'A forelimb and its flight specialisation are related but not the same comparison.']]);
